window.docContents[82].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Schur___s-lemma-for-algebraically-closed-fields/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Schur___s-lemma-for-algebraically-closed-fields--Schur___s-lemma-for-algebraically-closed-fields--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"Every linear endomorphism of a finite-dimensional simple module over an algebraically closed\nfield is scalar multiplication.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Corollary2.3.10; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_proof_Corollary2.3.10/Derived2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_proof_Corollary2.3.12/Derived2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Schur's lemma for algebraically closed fields\u0009Schur's lemma for algebraically closed fields\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Schur___s-lemma-for-algebraically-closed-fields/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Schur___s-lemma-for-algebraically-closed-fields--Schur___s-lemma-for-algebraically-closed-fields--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Homomorphism-of-Lie-algebras/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___9___-Lie-algebras--Homomorphism-of-Lie-algebras--Homomorphism-of-Lie-algebras--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Declaration: LieHom\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.9.6; role=supporting\n\nAn auxiliary type depending on two Lie algebras over a commutative ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.9.6; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras\u0009Homomorphism of Lie algebras\u0009Homomorphism of Lie algebras\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Homomorphism-of-Lie-algebras/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___9___-Lie-algebras--Homomorphism-of-Lie-algebras--Homomorphism-of-Lie-algebras--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem--Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem--Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"An equivalence after tensor extension yields an equivalence before tensor extension under finite-dimensionality hypotheses.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.8.4; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.8.4/Derived3; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.8. The Krull-Schmidt theorem\u0009Scalar extension and the Noether-Deuring theorem\u0009Scalar extension and the Noether-Deuring theorem\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem--Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem--Scalar-extension-and-the-Noether-Deuring-theorem--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___10___-Frobenius-determinant/Proof-of-Theorem-4___10___2-_LPAR_Frobenius-determinant-factorization_RPAR_/#Chapter4___Discussion_proof_Theorem4___10___2":{"contents":"Now we are ready to proceed to the proof of Theorem 4.10.2.\n\nProof. Let V = \\mathbb{C}[G] be the regular representation of G. Consider the operator-valued polynomial\n\nL(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_{g \\in G} x_g \\rho(g),\nwhere \\rho(g) \\in \\mathrm{End}\\, V is induced by g. The action of L(\\mathbf{x}) on an element h \\in G is\n\nL(\\mathbf{x})h = \\sum_{g \\in G} x_g \\rho(g) h = \\sum_{g \\in G} x_g g h = \\sum_{z \\in G} x_{zh^{-1}} z.\n\nSo the matrix of the linear operator L(\\mathbf{x}) in the basis g_1, g_2, \\ldots, g_n is X_G with permuted columns and hence has the same determinant up to sign.\n\nFurther, by Maschke's theorem, we have\n\n\\det_V L(\\mathbf{x}) = \\prod_{i=1}^{r} (\\det_{V_i} L(\\mathbf{x}))^{\\dim V_i},\n\nwhere V_i are the irreducible representations of G. We set P_i = \\det_{V_i} L(\\mathbf{x}). Let \\{e_{im}\\} be bases of V_i and let E_{i,jk} \\in \\mathrm{End}\\, V_i be the matrix units in these bases. Then \\{E_{i,jk}\\} is a basis of \\mathbb{C}[G] and\n\nL(\\mathbf{x})|_{V_i} = \\sum_{j,k} y_{i,jk} E_{i,jk},\n\nwhere y_{i,jk} are new coordinates on \\mathbb{C}[G] related to x_g by a linear transformation. Then\n\nP_i(\\mathbf{x}) = \\det|_{V_i} L(\\mathbf{x}) = \\det(y_{i,jk}).\n\nHence, P_i are irreducible (by Lemma 4.10.3) and not proportional to each other (as they depend on different collections of variables y_{i,jk}). The theorem is proved. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.10. Frobenius determinant\u0009Proof of Theorem 4.10.2 (Frobenius determinant factorization)","header":"Proof of Theorem 4.10.2 (Frobenius determinant factorization)","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___10___-Frobenius-determinant/Proof-of-Theorem-4___10___2-_LPAR_Frobenius-determinant-factorization_RPAR_/#Chapter4___Discussion_proof_Theorem4___10___2"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___16___-Problems/Branching-rules___-restriction-and-induction-for-S_n-representations/#Chapter5___Problem5___16___1":{"contents":"Problem 5.16.1. For a Young diagram \\mu, let A(\\mu) be the set of Young diagrams obtained by adding a square to \\mu, and let R(\\mu) be the set of Young diagrams obtained by removing a square from \\mu.\n\n(a) Show that \\operatorname{Res}_{S_{n-1}}^{S_n} V_\\mu = \\bigoplus_{\\lambda \\in R(\\mu)} V_\\lambda.\n\n(b) Show that \\operatorname{Ind}_{S_{n-1}}^{S_n} V_\\mu = \\bigoplus_{\\lambda \\in A(\\mu)} V_\\lambda.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.16. Problems\u0009Branching rules: restriction and induction for S_n representations","header":"Branching rules: restriction and induction for S_n representations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___16___-Problems/Branching-rules___-restriction-and-induction-for-S_n-representations/#Chapter5___Problem5___16___1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___4___6/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___4___6--Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___4___6--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"For a simple finite-group representation, coprimality of the conjugacy-class size and dimension forces a zero character value or scalar action.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_before_Lemma5.4.5; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5.4.4; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5.4.6; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.4.4; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.4. Burnside's theorem\u0009Completion of proof of Theorem 5.4.6\u0009Completion of proof of Theorem 5.4.6\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___4___6/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem--Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___4___6--Completion-of-proof-of-Theorem-5___4___6--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Proof-of-Theorem-5___4___4/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___4___4":{"contents":"Proof of Theorem 5.4.4. Let \\dim V = n. Let \\varepsilon_1, \\varepsilon_2, \\ldots, \\varepsilon_n be the eigenvalues of \\rho_V(g). They are roots of unity, so \\chi_V(g) is an algebraic integer. Also, by Proposition 5.3.2, \\frac{1}{n}|C|\\chi_V(g) is an algebraic integer. Since \\gcd(n, |C|) = 1, there exist integers a, b such that a|C| + bn = 1. This implies that\n\n\\frac{a|C|\\chi_V(g)}{n} + b\\chi_V(g) = \\frac{\\chi_V(g)}{n} = \\frac{1}{n}(\\varepsilon_1 + \\cdots + \\varepsilon_n)\n\nis an algebraic integer. Thus, by Lemma 5.4.5, we get that either \\varepsilon_1 = \\cdots = \\varepsilon_n or \\varepsilon_1 + \\cdots + \\varepsilon_n = \\chi_V(g) = 0. In the first case, since \\rho_V(g) is diagonalizable, it must be scalar. In the second case, \\chi_V(g) = 0. The theorem is proved. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.4. Burnside's theorem\u0009Proof of Theorem 5.4.4","header":"Proof of Theorem 5.4.4","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Proof-of-Theorem-5___4___4/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___4___4"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Example-6___2___4___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A3/#Chapter6___Example6___2___4___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.2. Indecomposable representations of the quivers $A_1$, $A_2$, $A_3$\u0009Example 6.2.4: Indecomposable representations of A3\u0009Example 6.2.4: Indecomposable representations of A3","header":"Formalization","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Example-6___2___4___-Indecomposable-representations-of-A3/#Chapter6___Example6___2___4___formalization"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/#chapter-08___section-8-2":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra","header":"8.2. Tor and Ext functors","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/#chapter-08___section-8-2"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Homological-dimension-of-a-ring/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___4___-Homological-dimension--Homological-dimension-of-a-ring--Homological-dimension-of-a-ring--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary extended-natural-valued invariant of a ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.4.3; role=primary]\n\nA second auxiliary extended-natural-valued invariant of a ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.4.3; role=primary]\n\nA third auxiliary extended-natural-valued invariant of a ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.4.3; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.4. Homological dimension\u0009Homological dimension of a ring\u0009Homological dimension of a ring\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Homological-dimension-of-a-ring/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___4___-Homological-dimension--Homological-dimension-of-a-ring--Homological-dimension-of-a-ring--Formalization--Primary-declarations"}});